      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Vulvar pain is a common presenting complaint in dermatology consultations arising from numerous possible causes. Where such pain is mechanical, resulting from stretching of the vulva, particularly during sexual intercourse, the patient should be checked for anatomical features that could account for the pain, localised anatomical adhesions or sclerosis of the fourchette, and amenable to treatment by vulvoperineoplasty."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "A retrospective study of all patients undergoing vulvoperineoplasty for vulvar pain in the fourchette was carried out between 1.1.1993 and 1.1.2003."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Fifteen successive patients were included in the study. The mean rate of sexual intercourse doubled between the pre-operative period and the post-operative period, rising from 5 (0 to 30) to 9.8 (3 to 30). Mean pain intensity during intercourse on a scale of 1 to 10 fell from 7.6 (5 to 9) before surgery to 1.6 (0 to 5) after surgery. None of the patients experienced any sequelae. All patients except one (who was no longer sexually active), expressed willingness to undergo vulvoperineoplasty again if necessary."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Vulvoperineoplasty is a simple procedure and proved useful for female patients presenting vulvar pain and adhesions or sclerosis of the fourchette."
        }
      ]},
